<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dorothea von Biron</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a>–<a href="/years/1862">1862</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Dorothea, Duchess of Sagan, Dorothée de Talleyrand-Périgord, Duchess of Dino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dorothea von Biron was the youngest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-von-biron">Peter von Biron</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>. She married Edmond de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand-Périgord</a>, nephew of the French statesman Talleyrand, and became the elder Talleyrand's companion and hostess, notably at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Known as the Duchess of Dino, she later lived in Silesia and became Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Sagan</a> in the 1840s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dorothea-von-biron.jpg" alt="Dorothea von Biron" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Lefèvre · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grafiene-Doroteja_by_Robert_Lef%C3%A8vre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
